# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the opt-in TSan profiles (t-tsan, tsan-ci,
# dev-tsan). Wired via TSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=... in the Makefile targets.
#
# Format (one per line, matched against report stack frames / module names):
#   race:<function-or-source-file>   — drop race reports whose stack contains it
#   deadlock:<function>              — drop lock-order reports whose stack contains it
#   mutex:<function>                 — drop mutex-misuse reports whose stack contains it
#   thread:<function>                — drop reports on threads started there
#   called_from_lib:<module>         — drop reports called from an uninstrumented lib
#
# HARD RULE: every active entry below must be CONFIRMED benign and carry a
# justification comment (why the race cannot corrupt state). Never add an
# entry to hide an untriaged report — triage first, fix forward, suppress
# only what is provably benign. Current triage state: docs/dev/BUILD.md
# "ThreadSanitizer profiles" (known-good-as-of note).
#
# Project code has no active suppressions — the first sweep's one finding was
# fixed in code (atomic publication of thread_liveness_child.id), not suppressed.
#
# Vendored Tor's thread pool, vendor/tor/src/lib/evloop/workqueue.c. The Tor
# archive is not instrumented, so TSan records only the libc call and its
# immediate caller: the pool's own functions never appear in these reports, and
# these two frames are the narrowest names they carry.
#
# threadpool_start_threads holds pool->control_lock, takes pool->lock, then
# waits on the condition with control_lock while holding lock. Only the thread
# creating the pool ever nests the two; workers take them one at a time, so no
# second thread can complete the cycle.
deadlock:tor_cond_wait
# control_lock is a semaphore on purpose: the pool's creator leaves it locked
# and the last worker to exit unlocks it, which is how threadpool_stop_threads
# waits for the workers to finish.
#
# This row is deliberately broad: it drops every mutex-misuse report that passes
# through Tor's unlock wrapper, not only the pool's. No narrower name exists while
# libtor.a is uninstrumented.
mutex:tor_mutex_release
